1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,384
A team that depends on an open-source gateway
finds a bug, fixes it in an afternoon

2
00:00:09,424 --> 00:00:13,239
and opens a pull request. Three
weeks later nothing has happened.

3
00:00:13,279 --> 00:00:18,104
Meanwhile the project's main has moved,
the pull request shows a conflict, and the

4
00:00:18,144 --> 00:00:21,722
engineer's fork says it is "two
commits ahead, forty behind".

5
00:00:21,762 --> 00:00:26,646
Nobody on the team can say how the fork got
ahead of a project they never had write

6
00:00:26,686 --> 00:00:27,239
access to.

7
00:00:27,579 --> 00:00:31,416
Both problems have causes you can
name. One is in the commit graph.

8
00:00:31,456 --> 00:00:35,408
The other is in how the pull request
spends a maintainer's attention.

9
00:00:35,448 --> 00:00:40,037
Keep that "two commits ahead" in mind.
One number in a terminal will explain it.

10
00:00:41,877 --> 00:00:45,718
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:45,758 --> 00:00:50,298
This is Part 8, professional practice.
Parts 1 to 7 gave you mechanisms.

12
00:00:50,338 --> 00:00:55,451
This part is about agreements between
people, and the claim of Chapter 27 is that

13
00:00:55,491 --> 00:01:00,667
every such agreement can be read off two
facts you already have: a branch is a ref

14
00:01:00,707 --> 00:01:04,738
that names a commit, and a release
is a tag that names a commit.

15
00:01:04,778 --> 00:01:09,891
A commit is one saved snapshot of the
project, and a ref is a name that points at

16
00:01:09,931 --> 00:01:10,145
one.

17
00:01:10,485 --> 00:01:14,432
In video 127 you carried one
change through the fork workflow.

18
00:01:14,472 --> 00:01:19,834
A fork is your own copy of a project's
repository on GitHub, and a pull request asks

19
00:01:19,874 --> 00:01:25,044
the project to take your commits. Today
the project doesn't stand still while you

20
00:01:25,084 --> 00:01:28,838
wait. Upstream, the project's
own repository, moves, twice.

21
00:01:29,178 --> 00:01:33,821
You sync the fork's main, update your
branch once by rebase and once by merge,

22
00:01:33,861 --> 00:01:38,504
answer a review round, and see what a
squash merge does to the question "is my

23
00:01:38,544 --> 00:01:43,307
branch merged?". Then we change sides and
look at the same pull request from the

24
00:01:43,347 --> 00:01:44,448
maintainer's chair.

25
00:01:44,788 --> 00:01:49,578
A note on layers, which the chapter makes
at its start: branches, tags, merges and

26
00:01:49,618 --> 00:01:53,701
rebases are Git. Forks, pull requests
and the merge button are GitHub.

27
00:01:53,741 --> 00:01:56,292
A workflow is a convention
that people hold.

28
00:01:58,132 --> 00:01:59,710
Here's what you'll walk away with.

29
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:01,463
After this video you can:

30
00:02:01,803 --> 00:02:06,602
Set up the three repositories and two remotes
of the fork workflow from the command

31
00:02:06,642 --> 00:02:06,894
line.

32
00:02:07,234 --> 00:02:11,921
Sync a fork's default branch in three ways
and update a topic branch in two, and say

33
00:02:11,961 --> 00:02:13,384
what each does to history.

34
00:02:13,724 --> 00:02:17,031
Repair a fork whose main has stray
commits without losing them.

35
00:02:17,371 --> 00:02:21,704
Respond to a review round on a rebased
branch so that the reviewer can see what

36
00:02:21,744 --> 00:02:22,147
changed.

37
00:02:22,487 --> 00:02:27,041
Apply the etiquette of section 27.4
as contributor and as maintainer.

38
00:02:28,881 --> 00:02:33,501
Why the fork workflow exists. A project
can't give write access to everyone who

39
00:02:33,541 --> 00:02:37,276
might send a fix. So you never
push to the project's repository.

40
00:02:37,316 --> 00:02:42,054
You push to a server-side copy that you
own, and you ask the project to take your

41
00:02:42,094 --> 00:02:43,882
commits through a pull request.

42
00:02:44,222 --> 00:02:48,379
What it is, precisely. Three
repositories, each with its own refs.

43
00:02:48,719 --> 00:02:53,127
You fetch from upstream, you fetch and
push to your fork, and you do the work in

44
00:02:53,167 --> 00:02:53,738
your clone.

45
00:02:57,078 --> 00:03:02,547
Inside .git. Nothing is new. refs/remotes/upstream/*
and refs/remotes/origin/* are

46
00:03:02,587 --> 00:03:07,788
two sets of remote-tracking refs, filled
by two fetch refspecs in .git/config.

47
00:03:07,828 --> 00:03:13,163
A remote-tracking ref records where a
branch was on a server at your last fetch.

48
00:03:13,203 --> 00:03:16,992
The pull request doesn't exist
in your repository at all.

49
00:03:17,332 --> 00:03:22,174
When upstream moves. There are two different
things to keep current, and people mix

50
00:03:22,214 --> 00:03:22,645
them up.

51
00:03:22,985 --> 00:03:27,973
The first is the fork's default branch. It
should be an exact copy of the project's

52
00:03:28,013 --> 00:03:32,881
main. If you never commit on it, updating
it is always a fast-forward: the branch

53
00:03:32,921 --> 00:03:37,970
name moves ahead, and no new commit is made.
That's the rule to remember from today:

54
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:42,696
never commit on the fork's main. It should
only ever fast-forward to upstream.

55
00:03:43,036 --> 00:03:47,376
The second is your feature branch. It
contains your commits on top of an old

56
00:03:47,416 --> 00:03:51,986
upstream commit. Updating it means either
replaying your commits on the new tip,

57
00:03:52,026 --> 00:03:55,329
which is a rebase, or merging
the new tip into the branch.

58
00:03:55,669 --> 00:04:00,584
GitHub documents three ways to do the first.
Two run on the server and are described

59
00:04:00,624 --> 00:04:04,242
from the documentation. The third
is plain Git, and we run it.

60
00:04:04,582 --> 00:04:09,527
One trap in the middle row: gh repo sync
without an argument updates the local

61
00:04:09,567 --> 00:04:12,850
repository instead. The
destination is the argument.

62
00:04:13,190 --> 00:04:17,707
Rebase or merge for the branch. Which to
use is the project's decision: read its

63
00:04:17,747 --> 00:04:22,434
contribution guide. Where it is silent, the
textbook's rule is: rebase freely until

64
00:04:22,474 --> 00:04:27,104
review starts and add commits afterwards,
because a reviewer who has read a commit

65
00:04:27,144 --> 00:04:29,780
should be able to trust
that it hasn't changed.

66
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:35,006
Etiquette, in one sentence. A maintainer
is a person who decides what the project

67
00:04:35,046 --> 00:04:39,689
accepts. A maintainer's scarce resource
is attention, and every convention of

68
00:04:39,729 --> 00:04:44,372
open-source contribution is a way of
spending less of it per accepted change.

69
00:04:44,712 --> 00:04:47,841
Before you write code: read
the contribution guide.

70
00:04:47,881 --> 00:04:52,875
It tells you the branch to target, the
merge method, the test command, the commit

71
00:04:52,915 --> 00:04:57,908
message convention and whether a sign-off
or a contributor agreement is required.

72
00:04:57,948 --> 00:05:02,942
GitHub shows a repository's CONTRIBUTING
file when someone opens an issue or pull

73
00:05:02,982 --> 00:05:07,727
request, and since the seventh of August
2025 in the repository's tab bar and

74
00:05:07,767 --> 00:05:08,224
sidebar.

75
00:05:08,564 --> 00:05:13,290
And open an issue first for anything large.
A pull request nobody asked for forces

76
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:17,939
the maintainer to choose between reviewing
work that doesn't fit the roadmap and

77
00:05:17,979 --> 00:05:22,531
rejecting work somebody spent a week on.
An issue costs both sides ten minutes.

78
00:05:22,871 --> 00:05:27,984
When you open the pull request: one logical
change, because a small, self-contained

79
00:05:28,024 --> 00:05:31,648
change is one a stranger can
evaluate. Say what you tested.

80
00:05:31,688 --> 00:05:35,808
For an ML change, which evaluation
set, which metric, which commit.

81
00:05:35,848 --> 00:05:39,347
Leave "allow edits by maintainers"
on for a fork you own.

82
00:05:39,387 --> 00:05:44,563
And expect checks to behave differently:
workflows on a pull request from a fork run

83
00:05:44,603 --> 00:05:49,344
without the project's secrets, and in a
public repository the default is that

84
00:05:49,384 --> 00:05:53,939
first-time contributors need a maintainer's
approval before workflows run.

85
00:05:54,279 --> 00:05:59,017
A red check that needs a secret isn't your
bug. Say so in the pull request instead

86
00:05:59,057 --> 00:06:00,124
of pushing guesses.

87
00:06:00,464 --> 00:06:05,440
While it is in review: answer every comment,
and don't force-push over a review in

88
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:10,395
progress unless the project asks for it.
Don't open a second pull request because

89
00:06:10,435 --> 00:06:15,227
the first is quiet. Ask once, after a
reasonable wait, in the place the project

90
00:06:15,267 --> 00:06:15,594
names.

91
00:06:15,934 --> 00:06:19,131
The maintainer's side. The
same economics in reverse.

92
00:06:19,471 --> 00:06:24,410
Read it as need and tool: rules told once,
review routed, main kept releasable.

93
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:30,230
A picture helps. The textbook's
analogy is a journal submission.

94
00:06:30,270 --> 00:06:35,317
You don't edit the journal. You send a
manuscript, reviewers ask for changes, you

95
00:06:35,357 --> 00:06:37,767
send a revision, and an editor decides.

96
00:06:38,107 --> 00:06:43,106
The analogy carries the etiquette well. A
manuscript on a topic the journal didn't

97
00:06:43,146 --> 00:06:47,654
ask for, three times the usual length,
with no statement of method, waits.

98
00:06:47,694 --> 00:06:49,067
So does a pull request.

99
00:06:49,407 --> 00:06:54,687
It breaks at storage. On GitHub your copy
and the journal's share one object store,

100
00:06:54,727 --> 00:06:59,686
a fork network, so what you push to your
fork can be reached from the upstream

101
00:06:59,726 --> 00:07:03,788
repository by commit ID. A manuscript
in your drawer is private.

102
00:07:03,828 --> 00:07:06,095
A commit on your public fork is not.

103
00:07:06,435 --> 00:07:11,603
For the Git side, hold this picture, with
its three labels: your local main is only

104
00:07:11,643 --> 00:07:16,560
a relay. First, it fast-forwards to
upstream/main. Then a push carries the same

105
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:20,764
commit on to origin/main. It never
contributes a commit of its own.

106
00:07:20,804 --> 00:07:26,035
The moment it has one, the relay is broken,
and every later pull request branch that

107
00:07:26,075 --> 00:07:28,607
starts from it carries that commit along.

108
00:07:30,447 --> 00:07:35,858
Follow the numbers. One, the fork. Two,
the clone. Three, a fetch from upstream.

109
00:07:35,898 --> 00:07:41,376
Four, a push to your fork. Five, the pull
request. Six and seven, the review asks

110
00:07:41,416 --> 00:07:45,396
for changes and you push again.
Eight, a maintainer merges.

111
00:07:45,736 --> 00:07:50,390
Notice what is missing: no arrow goes
from your clone up into the left box.

112
00:07:50,430 --> 00:07:54,771
You fetch from upstream. You never
push to it. The pull request's ref,

113
00:07:54,811 --> 00:07:59,841
refs/pull/57/head, lives in the upstream
repository, although the commits arrived

114
00:07:59,881 --> 00:08:00,968
through your fork.

115
00:08:02,808 --> 00:08:06,982
Into the lab, where two local repositories
play the project and your fork.

116
00:08:07,322 --> 00:08:12,976
Step 1: clone the fork, name the upstream.
🟢 SAFE: adding a remote and fetching add

117
00:08:13,016 --> 00:08:14,142
refs and objects.

118
00:08:14,482 --> 00:08:19,713
Four refs name one commit, 4ebb785.
origin/main is your clone's record of the fork

119
00:08:19,753 --> 00:08:24,020
and upstream/main its record of the
project. They will drift apart.

120
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:29,422
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In any
clone you have, type git remote -v.

121
00:08:29,462 --> 00:08:32,318
It only reads. Count the names. I'll wait.

122
00:08:37,158 --> 00:08:42,909
One name, usually origin, is an ordinary
clone. Two names, origin and upstream, is

123
00:08:42,949 --> 00:08:44,181
the fork workflow.

124
00:08:44,521 --> 00:08:48,078
Step 2: branch from upstream,
commit, publish on the fork.

125
00:08:48,118 --> 00:08:53,102
🟢 SAFE for the new branch and the commit.
🟡 CAUTION for the push, which creates a

126
00:08:53,142 --> 00:08:54,838
new branch on your own fork.

127
00:08:55,178 --> 00:08:59,001
Predict: after these three commands,
what is the upstream branch of

128
00:08:59,041 --> 00:09:03,844
fix/suspended-tenant? Careful: the upstream
branch of a branch is the one git status

129
00:09:03,884 --> 00:09:07,476
compares it with, not the upstream
repository. Say it out loud.

130
00:09:12,316 --> 00:09:17,535
Read the two "set up to track" lines. The
switch made upstream/main the upstream of

131
00:09:17,575 --> 00:09:21,021
the new branch, which is the
right base for comparison.

132
00:09:21,061 --> 00:09:26,090
The push with -u then replaced it with
origin/fix/suspended-tenant, which is the

133
00:09:26,130 --> 00:09:31,223
right destination for pushes. After this,
git status compares you with your fork,

134
00:09:31,263 --> 00:09:36,102
not with the project. To compare with
the project you ask explicitly: git log

135
00:09:36,142 --> 00:09:38,003
--oneline upstream/main..HEAD.

136
00:09:38,343 --> 00:09:40,370
Step 3: upstream moves.

137
00:09:40,710 --> 00:09:45,369
One and zero. One commit on the left
side, upstream/main, that the right side,

138
00:09:45,409 --> 00:09:50,370
origin/main, lacks, and none the other way.
That second zero is what makes the sync

139
00:09:50,410 --> 00:09:55,190
safe. If it were a two, you would be
looking at the fork from the hook: somebody

140
00:09:55,230 --> 00:09:58,443
committed on the fork's main.
That's how it got ahead.

141
00:09:58,783 --> 00:10:01,598
Step 4: sync the fork's
main with plain Git.

142
00:10:01,938 --> 00:10:06,823
--ff-only is the guard. If you had committed
on main by mistake, the merge would

143
00:10:06,863 --> 00:10:11,994
stop instead of creating a merge commit that
exists only in your fork and that every

144
00:10:12,034 --> 00:10:16,242
later pull request would carry. Zero
and zero: the relay is in order.

145
00:10:16,582 --> 00:10:22,376
Step 5: update the branch, way 1, by rebase.
🟡 CAUTION for git rebase: it rewrites

146
00:10:22,416 --> 00:10:26,786
local commits. Predict: will a
plain git push work afterwards?

147
00:10:26,826 --> 00:10:27,498
Yes or no?

148
00:10:32,338 --> 00:10:36,151
🔴 DANGEROUS: git push
--force-with-lease --force-if-includes.

149
00:10:36,191 --> 00:10:41,077
The five answers before it runs. What it
changes: the branch on the fork, to a

150
00:10:41,117 --> 00:10:46,257
commit that doesn't descend from the old
one. What it can destroy: commits on that

151
00:10:46,297 --> 00:10:51,373
server branch that aren't in your clone,
for example a fix a maintainer pushed to

152
00:10:51,413 --> 00:10:52,952
your pull request branch.

153
00:10:53,292 --> 00:10:58,686
Preview: git fetch origin, then git log
HEAD..origin/fix/suspended-tenant lists what

154
00:10:58,726 --> 00:11:04,056
would be dropped. Recovery: push the old
tip back from any clone that still has it.

155
00:11:04,096 --> 00:11:09,361
Your own old tip is in the branch reflog.
When it is appropriate: on a branch that

156
00:11:09,401 --> 00:11:11,496
only you push to, after a rebase.

157
00:11:11,835 --> 00:11:17,674
The rebase replaced commit a83a713 with
3560caf: same change, new parent, new ID.

158
00:11:17,714 --> 00:11:22,753
The fork still had the old commit,
so the plain push was rejected as a

159
00:11:22,793 --> 00:11:23,987
non-fast-forward.

160
00:11:24,327 --> 00:11:29,426
As a graph, with labels. First, the rebase
copies your commit onto the new tip, and

161
00:11:29,466 --> 00:11:33,636
the fork's copy stays on the old
one. Then the forced push moves it.

162
00:11:33,676 --> 00:11:38,527
The lease makes the forced push succeed
only if the fork's branch is where your

163
00:11:38,567 --> 00:11:43,727
clone last saw it, and --force-if-includes
only if you have integrated what you last

164
00:11:43,767 --> 00:11:43,975
saw.

165
00:11:44,315 --> 00:11:48,565
Step 6: upstream moves
again. Way 2, by merge.

166
00:11:48,905 --> 00:11:52,942
No commit changed its ID, so the
push is an ordinary fast-forward.

167
00:11:53,282 --> 00:11:56,946
The price is the merge commit 44d750c
in the middle of your branch.

168
00:11:57,286 --> 00:12:01,743
Stay on the fourth row. If the project
asks you to rebase during review, the

169
00:12:01,783 --> 00:12:06,358
reviewer can't see from the forced push
what changed between the two versions.

170
00:12:06,398 --> 00:12:11,328
git range-diff, from video 58, is the tool
that compares the old series with the new

171
00:12:11,368 --> 00:12:16,002
one. Say in the pull request that you
rebased and what changed, so the reviewer

172
00:12:16,042 --> 00:12:18,251
doesn't have to read everything again.

173
00:12:18,591 --> 00:12:24,117
Quick quiz. Review has started, the project's
guide is silent, and upstream moves

174
00:12:24,157 --> 00:12:28,928
again. A, rebase and force-push, or
B, merge upstream into the branch?

175
00:12:28,968 --> 00:12:29,753
Your answer?

176
00:12:34,593 --> 00:12:39,261
B, by the textbook's rule: rebase freely
until review starts, and add commits

177
00:12:39,301 --> 00:12:43,847
afterwards. A merge changes no commit
ID, so review comments stay attached.

178
00:12:44,187 --> 00:12:47,821
Step 7: the review round. The
reviewer asks for a test.

179
00:12:47,861 --> 00:12:51,762
After review has started, changes
are added, not rewritten.

180
00:12:52,102 --> 00:12:56,219
A plain push, and the pull request
updates because it follows the branch.

181
00:12:56,559 --> 00:13:01,533
Step 8: the maintainer squash-merges. The
lab imitates the merge button with git

182
00:13:01,573 --> 00:13:06,798
merge --squash in Asha's clone. GitHub
documents the result as one new commit on the

183
00:13:06,838 --> 00:13:11,373
base branch. A squash folds the commits
of a branch into that one commit.

184
00:13:11,413 --> 00:13:14,883
Predict: is your branch tip
an ancestor of the new main?

185
00:13:14,923 --> 00:13:15,886
Say it out loud.

186
00:13:20,726 --> 00:13:26,306
Exit status 1: not an ancestor. git branch
--merged tests the same reachability and

187
00:13:26,346 --> 00:13:31,791
wouldn't list the branch. The empty git
diff --stat is the content test: main has

188
00:13:31,831 --> 00:13:33,552
everything the branch has.

189
00:13:33,892 --> 00:13:39,081
One line contradicts a rule you may have
read: git branch -d deleted a branch that

190
00:13:39,121 --> 00:13:44,182
wasn't merged into main, with a warning.
The manual explains it: -d requires the

191
00:13:44,222 --> 00:13:49,283
branch to be "fully merged in its upstream
branch, or in HEAD if no upstream was

192
00:13:49,323 --> 00:13:54,320
set". The upstream here is
origin/fix/suspended-tenant, which has every commit.

193
00:13:54,660 --> 00:14:00,051
Now the reason, as a graph. The squash
commit 16e9e75 has one parent and no link to

194
00:14:00,091 --> 00:14:04,304
your commits, so your branch tip
147ed67 isn't an ancestor of it.

195
00:14:04,344 --> 00:14:09,735
"Merged" in Git means "reachable from", and
the merge method discarded the ancestry

196
00:14:09,775 --> 00:14:11,109
and kept the content.

197
00:14:11,449 --> 00:14:17,480
This is the root-cause box of section
27.3. Read its fix line: test content, not

198
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:18,163
ancestry.

199
00:14:18,503 --> 00:14:23,426
Now the same cycle in your own practice
repository, following Lab 21.1, Part B.

200
00:14:23,466 --> 00:14:28,514
The GitHub interface changes. The lab's
text and the linked documentation are the

201
00:14:28,554 --> 00:14:32,345
reference, and no GitHub output
was captured for this course.

202
00:14:32,385 --> 00:14:37,496
Run it in your normal shell, not in
labs/shell, because the lab shell switches off

203
00:14:37,536 --> 00:14:41,202
the system configuration where
the credential helper lives.

204
00:14:41,542 --> 00:14:46,611
As the contributor: fork the practice
repository and clone the fork, and check with

205
00:14:46,651 --> 00:14:51,719
git remote -v that two remotes exist. Create
the branch from upstream/main, commit,

206
00:14:51,759 --> 00:14:56,273
push to origin, and open the pull
request against the upstream repository.

207
00:14:56,613 --> 00:15:00,393
On the pull request page, find
three things by their function.

208
00:15:00,433 --> 00:15:03,966
First, the base and the head,
which name two repositories.

209
00:15:04,006 --> 00:15:07,231
Second, the control that
allows edits by maintainers.

210
00:15:07,271 --> 00:15:08,833
Third, the list of checks.

211
00:15:09,173 --> 00:15:12,170
Then exchange the roles, so
that you're the maintainer.

212
00:15:12,210 --> 00:15:15,207
Open the pull request as the
owner of the organization.

213
00:15:15,547 --> 00:15:20,774
Read it the way section 27.4 says a maintainer
does: is it one logical change, does

214
00:15:20,814 --> 00:15:25,852
it say what was tested, does it target
the branch your contribution guide names?

215
00:15:25,892 --> 00:15:30,675
Merge it, and before you confirm a
squash, read the proposed commit message.

216
00:15:31,015 --> 00:15:35,546
Then go back to the contributor's clone,
sync main with the fast-forward relay, and

217
00:15:35,586 --> 00:15:37,363
delete the branch in both places.

218
00:15:39,203 --> 00:15:40,851
Five mistakes to watch for.

219
00:15:41,191 --> 00:15:45,996
Committing on the fork's main. Root cause:
the fork's default branch is treated as a

220
00:15:46,036 --> 00:15:50,206
working branch, so it stops being a
copy of upstream and can no longer be

221
00:15:50,246 --> 00:15:51,071
fast-forwarded.

222
00:15:51,411 --> 00:15:55,822
Starting the topic branch from the
fork's main instead of upstream/main.

223
00:15:55,862 --> 00:16:00,891
Root cause: nothing updates the fork for
you, so its main is an old commit and the

224
00:16:00,931 --> 00:16:02,560
pull request starts behind.

225
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:06,791
Asking "is it merged?" with an
ancestry test after a squash merge.

226
00:16:06,831 --> 00:16:11,139
Root cause: the merge method discarded
the ancestry and kept the content.

227
00:16:11,479 --> 00:16:15,999
Force-pushing over a review in progress.
Root cause: the reviewer's comments are

228
00:16:16,039 --> 00:16:20,387
anchored to commits that the rebase
replaced, so their work has to be redone.

229
00:16:20,727 --> 00:16:23,955
Pushing guesses at a red
check on a fork pull request.

230
00:16:23,995 --> 00:16:28,857
Root cause: workflows from forks run without
the project's secrets, so a job that

231
00:16:28,897 --> 00:16:31,217
needs one fails whatever the code does.

232
00:16:33,057 --> 00:16:38,073
Now, out of the lab. A model-evaluation
team uses an open-source tokenizer library

233
00:16:38,113 --> 00:16:42,945
and finds that it miscounts a class of
inputs. The engineer reads the project's

234
00:16:42,985 --> 00:16:44,487
contribution guide first.

235
00:16:44,827 --> 00:16:49,714
It asks for an issue before a pull request,
for a rebase onto main before review and

236
00:16:49,754 --> 00:16:54,114
added commits after, and for a sign-off.
She opens an issue with a ten-line

237
00:16:54,154 --> 00:16:58,865
reproduction. A maintainer answers the
next day and names the function to change.

238
00:16:59,205 --> 00:17:03,637
Her pull request is one commit and one
test, and its description says which

239
00:17:03,677 --> 00:17:07,513
evaluation set, which metric and
which commit she tested against.

240
00:17:07,553 --> 00:17:12,343
The check that publishes benchmark results
is red, because it needs a secret that

241
00:17:12,383 --> 00:17:15,443
fork runs don't get. She
says so in the description.

242
00:17:15,783 --> 00:17:20,735
When upstream moves, she rebases, pushes
with the lease, and writes one line saying

243
00:17:20,775 --> 00:17:25,245
that nothing but the base changed. The
pull request is merged in four days.

244
00:17:25,285 --> 00:17:30,116
The team's earlier attempt, a single large
pull request with a refactoring nobody

245
00:17:30,156 --> 00:17:31,860
had asked for, is still open.

246
00:17:33,700 --> 00:17:41,265
Your turn. Do Exercise 21.5, Level 3,
"The fork that cannot be synced", in

247
00:17:41,305 --> 00:17:44,143
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

248
00:17:44,483 --> 00:17:49,791
Before you run any command in it, predict
what git rev-list --left-right --count

249
00:17:49,831 --> 00:17:55,339
upstream/main...origin/main will print for
a fork whose main has stray commits, and

250
00:17:55,379 --> 00:17:59,417
what git merge --ff-only
upstream/main will do in that state.

251
00:17:59,457 --> 00:18:04,565
Write down how you would keep the stray
commits before you repair the branch.

252
00:18:04,605 --> 00:18:08,375
Then do the exercise. Its
solution is in a separate file.

253
00:18:08,415 --> 00:18:10,180
Open it after your attempt.

254
00:18:10,520 --> 00:18:17,827
The challenge is Exercise 34.6, Level 5,
"The contributor who was never answered",

255
00:18:17,867 --> 00:18:20,784
in exercises/m32-m34-practice.md.

256
00:18:24,624 --> 00:18:29,404
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
explains both numbers from the graph: what

257
00:18:29,444 --> 00:18:34,224
"ahead" means for a branch that should
only relay, and why "behind" is the normal

258
00:18:34,264 --> 00:18:38,985
state of a fork nobody synced. It names
the read-only command that shows the two

259
00:18:39,025 --> 00:18:43,984
counts. It then repairs in an order that
can't lose work: the two commits get a name

260
00:18:44,024 --> 00:18:48,923
before main is moved. And it says what would
have stopped the mistake at the moment

261
00:18:48,963 --> 00:18:52,613
it was made, which is one option
on one command you saw today.

262
00:18:54,453 --> 00:18:56,276
Let's land this in your own words.

263
00:18:56,616 --> 00:19:01,440
The fork workflow has three repositories
and two remotes; you fetch from upstream

264
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:02,581
and push to origin.

265
00:19:02,921 --> 00:19:08,032
The fork's main is a relay that only
fast-forwards; git merge --ff-only

266
00:19:08,072 --> 00:19:11,296
upstream/main is both
the sync and the guard.

267
00:19:11,636 --> 00:19:16,631
A topic branch is updated by rebase, which
needs a forced push with a lease, or by

268
00:19:16,671 --> 00:19:20,192
merge, which needs a plain push
and leaves a merge commit.

269
00:19:20,532 --> 00:19:25,484
After a squash merge the branch is not merged
by ancestry; test content, then delete

270
00:19:25,524 --> 00:19:27,267
the branch and never reuse it.

271
00:19:27,607 --> 00:19:32,844
A maintainer's scarce resource is attention:
contribution guide first, issue first,

272
00:19:32,884 --> 00:19:35,388
one logical change, say what you tested.

273
00:19:37,228 --> 00:19:42,695
Read sections 27.1 to 27.4. Then read the
CONTRIBUTING file of one project you use

274
00:19:42,735 --> 00:19:48,001
and note what it asks before a first pull
request: the target branch, the merge

275
00:19:48,041 --> 00:19:52,972
method, the test command, the message
convention, a sign-off or agreement.

276
00:19:53,312 --> 00:19:58,301
Today you kept a fork in step with a moving
project, and you sat in the maintainer's

277
00:19:58,341 --> 00:20:03,271
chair. Practise the relay once in the lab.
In the next video, branch names turn out

278
00:20:03,311 --> 00:20:07,163
to be conventions, and four branching
models are drawn as graphs.

279
00:20:07,203 --> 00:20:10,277
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

280
00:20:10,317 --> 00:20:11,714
See you in the next one.
